> If you have 24 density plots and want to avoid repetitive code, you can use a combination of `ggplot2` and `dplyr` to create the plots more efficiently. You can use a loop or function to dynamically generate the `geom_density()` layer for each group. 
> 
> Here’s an example of how to achieve that:
> 
> ```r
> # Load necessary libraries
> library(ggplot2)
> library(dplyr)
> 
> # Create a sample dataset
> set.seed(123)
> df <- data.frame(
>   x = c(rnorm(100, 0, 1), rnorm(100, 3, 1), rnorm(100, 6, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 9, 1), rnorm(100, 12, 1), rnorm(100, 15, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 18, 1), rnorm(100, 21, 1), rnorm(100, 24, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 27, 1), rnorm(100, 30, 1), rnorm(100, 33, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 36, 1), rnorm(100, 39, 1), rnorm(100, 42, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 45, 1), rnorm(100, 48, 1), rnorm(100, 51, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 54, 1), rnorm(100, 57, 1), rnorm(100, 60, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 63, 1), rnorm(100, 66, 1), rnorm(100, 69, 1),
>         rnorm(100, 72, 1)),
>   group = factor(rep(1:24, each = 100))
> )
> 
> # Create the ggplot object
> p <- ggplot(df, aes(x = x, y = ..density..)) + 
>   geom_blank()  # Placeholder to initialize the plot
> 
> # Loop to add density plots for each group
> for (i in 1:24) {
>   p <- p + 
>     geom_density(data = df %>% filter(group == i), 
>                  aes(y = ..density.. + i - 1),  # Offset each density plot
>                  fill = scales::hue_pal()(24)[i],  # Use different colors
>                  alpha = 0.6)
> }
> 
> # Customize the plot
> p + labs(x = "X", y = "Density Offset by Group") +
>     theme_minimal() +
>     scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(add = c(0.5, 0)))  # Ensure visibility
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation:
> 
> - **Dataset Creation**: The dataset contains 24 groups, where each group has 100 random points generated from different normal distributions.
> - **Looping through Groups**: We create a base ggplot object with `geom_blank()` and then use a loop to add `geom_density()` for each group. The `aes(y = ..density.. + i - 1)` offsets each density plot by `i - 1` to separate them vertically.
> - **Dynamic Colors**: We use `scales::hue_pal()(24)[i]` to assign different colors automatically to each density plot.
> - **Customization**: Finally, we add labels and adjust the y-axis to ensure all density plots are visible.
> 
> This approach allows you to efficiently create 24 density plots without repeating code and ensures everything is nicely organized!
