> To control the width of the density plots and reduce overlap, you can adjust the `adjust` parameter in the `geom_density()` function. This parameter allows you to change the bandwidth of the density estimate, which effectively controls how "wide" or "narrow" the density curve appears. A smaller value for `adjust` makes the density plots narrower, while a larger value makes them wider.
> 
> Here’s how you can incorporate this into your existing code:
> 
> ```r
> gp <- ggplot(subset(df, today), aes(x = soc_plugged)) +
>     geom_blank()  # Initialize the plot with a blank layer
> 
> for (hh in 0:23) {
>     gp <- gp +
>         geom_density(data = subset(df, today & hour == hh),
>                      aes(y = ..density.. + hh),  # Offset the density on the y-axis
>                      adjust = 0.5,  # Control the width of the density curves
>                      #fill=scales::hue_pal()(24)[hh + 1],
>                      alpha = 0.6)
> }
> 
> gp +
>   labs(x = "State of Charge (SoC)", y = "Density") +  # Add labels
>   theme_minimal()  # Optional: Apply a clean theme
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation of the Adjustments:
> - **`adjust` Parameter**: By setting `adjust = 0.5`, you make the density estimate narrower. You can experiment with this value:
>   - **Below 1**: Makes the density plots narrower (less overlap).
>   - **Equal to 1**: Uses the default bandwidth.
>   - **Above 1**: Makes the density plots wider.
> 
> Feel free to play with different values of `adjust` to find the width that works best for your visualization. This adjustment should help reduce the overlap between the density curves.
